One of the Nautilus developer was working to test new changes on a container. He wants to keep a backup of his changes to the container. 
A new request has been raised for the DevOps team to create a new image from this container. Below are more details about it:

a. Create an image ecommerce:nautilus on Application Server 2 from a container ubuntu_latest that is running on same server.

SOLUTION
To create a new image from a running container, use docker commit. This snapshots the container's current filesystem state into a new image.

🛠️ Commands to Execute
Run the following on Application Server 2 (App Server 2):

bash
# 1. (Optional) Confirm the container is running
sudo docker ps

# 2. Create the new image from the container
sudo docker commit ubuntu_latest ecommerce:nautilus
📋 Verification
Confirm the new image was created successfully:

bash
sudo docker images | grep ecommerce
Expected output:

text
ecommerce   nautilus   <new-image-id>   ...   <size>
You can also inspect the image's details (including the container it was created from) with:

bash
sudo docker inspect ecommerce:nautilus
💡 Important Notes
docker commit creates the image from the container's current state (including any file changes the developer made), so all in-container modifications are preserved in ecommerce:nautilus.

The source container ubuntu_latest must exist (running or stopped) on App Server 2 for the commit to succeed.

If you want to include a commit message (good practice for tracking), use:

bash
sudo docker commit -m "Backup of developer changes" ubuntu_latest ecommerce:nautilus
The container keeps running after the commit; docker commit does not stop or modify it.
